  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A generally unsettled pattern persists through \nFriday as a quasi-stationary front remains across the Northeast, \nresulting in multiple opportunities for showers and thunderstorms. \nWhile coverage should remain limited through Thursday, stronger \nforcing Friday may bring a better chance for showers and \nthunderstorms before drier weather arrives for the weekend.\n\nA mid-level ridge remains anchored across the south-central and \nsoutheastern CONUS, while a weaker ridge extends into the Canadian \nPrairies. This leaves the flow aloft relatively quasi-zonal, \nallowing shortwave energy to quickly traverse the continent into the \nnortheast. Given the lack of strong synoptic-scale forcing, shower \ndevelopment and coverage will remain difficult to pin down through \nThursday.\n\nToday will be mainly dry, with a weak surface low passing south of \nNew England. The highest PoPs, generally 10-20 percent, will be \nconfined to the immediate south coast and Islands during the \nmorning. Otherwise, a mix of sun and clouds is expected, although \nanother surge of wildfire smoke may result in hazy or milky skies. A \nmid-level shortwave trough will move through tonight into Thursday \nmorning, providing enough forcing for scattered showers and a few \nthunderstorms. Coverage should remain limited, however, with PoPs \ngenerally in the 20-30 percent range northwest of I-95 and I-495. \nThursday features a weak disturbance passing through the flow, with \nsimilar PoPs once again across the eastern half of southern New \nEngland.  \n\nA more notable change arrives Friday as the front's departure has \ntrended later. A stronger 500 mb shortwave trough and increasing \nforcing will bring a better chance for showers and thunderstorms, \nparticularly as the front finally pushes offshore. Behind the front, \nhigh pressure builds into the region for Saturday and Sunday, \nbringing a much drier airmass along with mostly sunny and \ncomfortable conditions. The next opportunity for more substantial \nrainfall arrives late Sunday into Monday as low pressure approaches \nfrom the northern Great Lakes.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures trend toward seasonable levels through \nthe end of the week, with lower humidity and more comfortable \nconditions expected this weekend.\n\nTemperatures aloft gradually cool through the end of the week, with \ndew points following suit. The result will be a noticeably cooler \nand more refreshing airmass by late week. The weekend is shaping up \nto be the most comfortable stretch of weather in quite some time, \nwith highs generally in the upper 70s across interior southern New \nEngland and lower 80s along the coastal plain. Dew points in the 50s \nwill make for much more comfortable conditions, while overnight lows \nfall into the 50s Saturday and Sunday mornings. Temperatures early \nnext week remain seasonable, though dew points may trend upward on \nMonday depending on the timing of the next frontal system.",
